# World Physical Geography: The Face of the Planet

> Before political maps there were physical ones: mountain walls that
> decided where empires stopped, rivers that decided where cities
> rose, deserts and straits that decided who traded with whom. Static
> geography — the fixed furniture of the Earth — remains the skeleton
> on which every other geography hangs.

## 1. Continents and Oceans: The Frame

[definition]
The **seven continents** in decreasing area: Asia, Africa, North
America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, Australia. The **five
oceans**: Pacific (largest, deeper than all land is high), Atlantic
(longest N–S), Indian (warmest, monsoon engine), Southern (circling
Antarctica), Arctic (smallest, shallowest, ice-capped).
[/definition]

Continental facts that anchor exam questions: Asia has both the
highest point (Everest, 8,849 m) and the lowest exposed land (Dead
Sea shore, about −430 m); Africa is the only continent crossed by
both tropics and the Equator; South America's Andes form the world's
longest mountain chain; Europe and Asia share the world's largest
landmass, Eurasia.

## 2. Mountains: The Great Systems

| System | Location | Claim to fame |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Himalayas | Asia (India–Nepal–Bhutan–China) | highest range; young fold mountains |
| Karakoram | Pakistan/China | K2, world's 2nd peak (8,611 m) |
| Andes | South America | longest continental chain (~7,000 km) |
| Rockies | North America | young fold system, parallel ranges |
| Alps | Europe | arc shaping climate of central Europe |
| Atlas | North-west Africa | Sahara's edge |
| Urals | Russia | Europe–Asia boundary; old, low |
| Great Dividing Range | Eastern Australia | oldest here; continental divide |
| Drakensberg | Southern Africa | basalt escarpment |

[example]
Fold mountains are young and high (Himalayas, Andes, Alps, Rockies) —
raised where plates collide; block mountains are fault-bounded (Vosges,
Black Forest, Sierra Nevada); residual mountains are worn-down ancient
blocks (Aravallis, Urals, Appalachians). "Which type is X?" questions
are simply this table read aloud.
[/example]

## 3. Plateaus and Plains

- **Tibetan Plateau** — the "roof of the world" (avg ~4,500 m), the
  largest and highest plateau; its heating drives the monsoon.
- **Deccan Plateau** — basaltic, lava-born, covering peninsular
  India.
- **Brazilian Plateau**; **Guiana Highlands** (Angel Falls, the
  world's highest waterfall at 979 m); **Katanga Plateau**
  (copper belt); **East African Plateau** (rift lakes).
- Great plains: **Indo-Gangetic** (alluvial, world's most extensive
  expanse of flat fertility), **West Siberian** (world's largest
  continuous plain), **Pampas** (Argentina's grasslands), **Prairies**
  (North America), **Veld** (South Africa), **Downs** (Australia) —
  the temperate grassland names themselves are a favourite quiz line.

## 4. Rivers: The Arteries

| River | Outflow | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Nile (~6,650 km) | Mediterranean | longest in Africa; Aswan dam |
| Amazon | Atlantic | largest by discharge; basin = half of South America |
| Yangtze | East China Sea | Asia's longest; Three Gorges |
| Mississippi–Missouri | Gulf of Mexico | drains 31 US states |
| Yenisei | Arctic Ocean | largest system flowing to the Arctic |
| Congo | Atlantic | crosses Equator twice |
| Danube | Black Sea | most countries (10) |
| Rhine | North Sea | Europe's busiest waterway |

Rivers ending in interior basins (endorheic) — the Volga to the
Caspian, the Amu Darya and Syr Darya to the Aral, the Okavango to its
delta — star in questions about closed drainage.

## 5. Deserts and Straits

**Hot deserts** ring the subtropics (Tropic of Cancer and Capricorn):
Sahara (largest hot desert), Arabian, Thar, Kalahari, Atacama (driest
— rain-shadow plus cold Humboldt current), Australian outback. **Cold
deserts**: Gobi, Patagonian. Rain shadows, cold coasts and
continentality — the three desert-making machines — explain their
positions on the map.

The straits that gate world shipping: **Hormuz** (Gulf oil, ~20% of
the world's petroleum), **Malacca** (China's energy lifeline),
**Bab el-Mandeb** (Suez feeder), **Gibraltar** (Atlantic–Mediterranean),
**Bosphorus–Dardanelles** (Black Sea), **Magellan/Drake** (southern
passage), **Bering** (Asia–America, 82 km apart), **Palk**
(India–Sri Lanka), **Sunda/Makassar** (Indonesia).

Canals complete the shortcuts: **Suez** (1869; Mediterranean–Red Sea,
Europe–Asia ~7,000 km saved), **Panama** (1914; Atlantic–Pacific,
rounding Cape Horn avoided), **Kiel** (Baltic–North Sea).

## 6. Islands, Peninsulas, Seas and Basins

- **Largest islands**: Greenland (first), New Guinea, Borneo,
  Madagascar, Baffin.
- **Great peninsulas**: Arabia (largest), Indian (Deccan), Indochina,
  Iberian, Balkan, Crimea, Scandinavia (fjord-carved).
- **Deepest points**: Mariana Trench's Challenger Deep (~11,000 m)
  in the western Pacific.
- **Largest seas**: Coral Sea, Arabian Sea, South China Sea;
  **largest lake** by area: Caspian; **deepest**: Baikal; **largest
  freshwater** by area: Lake Superior; Africa's largest: Victoria
  (source of the White Nile).
- **Cape routes** before Suez and Panama: Cape of Good Hope (rounding
  Africa), Cape Horn (rounding South America) — still the route for
  ships too big for the canals.

## 7. Why Physical Geography Matters

The mountains that block monsoon winds decide where it rains (Cherrapunji
against Leh's desert); the plains that hold alluvium decide where
grain — and density — concentrate; the straits and canals decide
whose navy and trade dominate; the plateaus that expose old rocks
decide where minerals (Katanga's copper, Deccan's manganese) lie.
Climate, agriculture, industry and geopolitics are downstream
consequences of this fixed stage — which is why every competitive
exam still begins the world's story with its shape.

## 8. Summary

Learn the frame first: seven continents and five oceans; then the
mountain systems (young fold vs old residual), the plateaus, the
great alluvial plains, the rivers with their outflows, the desert
belt and its three causes, and the straits–canal pairs that route
world trade. Superlatives (largest, longest, deepest, driest) are the
exam's favourite currency — each one is a single line in the tables
above.
